The data structure is the **Process Control Block (PCB)**, also called the process descriptor. The operating system keeps one for each process and saves into it the state of the process when it is switched out, so that it can be restored later.

Four pieces of information held in a PCB (any four):

1. the process identifier (PID);
2. the current process state (new, ready, running, blocked, terminated);
3. the program counter (address of the next instruction);
4. the contents of the CPU registers (saved context);
5. scheduling information such as the priority;
6. memory-management information (page table, base and limit);
7. a list of the open files and I/O devices in use;
8. accounting information (CPU time used).
